/* ---------- more questions, each with a model answer and an examiner's tip ---------- */
QB("biz", [
["1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between goods and services.","Goods are tangible, physical products|tangible|physical|can be touched","Services are intangible, performed for customers|intangible|cannot be touched|performed|haircut|banking",{model:"Goods are tangible products that can be touched, such as a phone. Services are intangible: they are performed for customers, such as a haircut.",tip:"Use the words tangible and intangible, with an example each."}],
["1.1","Explain",2,"Explain how a business can add value.","Value added is the selling price minus the cost of bought-in inputs|selling price|minus|difference|cost of inputs|bought in","By using branding, design, convenience or quality that customers will pay more for|branding|brand|design|convenience|quality|pay more",{model:"Added value is the difference between the selling price and the cost of the inputs. A business adds value through branding, design, convenience or quality that customers are willing to pay more for.",tip:"Define added value, then give a way to increase it."}],
["1.2","Outline",2,"Outline one advantage and one disadvantage of being a public limited company.","Advantage: it can raise large amounts of capital by selling shares|raise capital|large amounts of capital|sell shares|selling shares|stock exchange","Disadvantage: it risks a takeover and must publish its accounts, so owners lose some control|takeover|accounts|loss of control|lose control|divorce of ownership|scrutiny",{model:"A public limited company can raise large amounts of capital by selling shares on the stock exchange, but anyone can buy those shares, so it risks a hostile takeover, and it must publish its accounts.",tip:"Label the advantage and the disadvantage clearly."}],
["1.2","Outline",2,"Outline what a cooperative is.","A business owned and run by its members, such as workers or customers|owned by members|members|workers|customers","Profits are shared among members, often with one member, one vote|shared|one vote|democratic",{model:"A cooperative is owned and run by its members, such as its workers or customers. Profits are shared among the members, and decisions are democratic: one member, one vote.",tip:"Ownership by members is the defining feature."}],
["1.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a vision statement and a mission statement.","A vision statement sets out the long-term aspiration: what the business wants to become|long term|future|aspiration|become","A mission statement sets out its purpose and core values: why it exists and how it will act now|purpose|present|core values|why it exists",{model:"A vision statement describes the long-term aspiration, what the business wants to become. A mission statement describes its current purpose and values, why it exists and how it works towards the vision.",tip:"Vision = future; mission = present purpose."}],
["1.3","Explain",2,"Explain why a business might change its objectives.","External changes such as a recession or new competitors|recession|competition|competitors|economy|external","Internal changes such as new leadership, growth or a crisis|new leader|new management|growth|internal|crisis",{model:"Objectives may change because of external factors, such as a recession forcing a focus on survival, or internal ones, such as new leaders with different priorities.",tip:"Give an external and an internal reason."}],
["1.4","Define",2,"Define stakeholder.","An individual or group with an interest in a business|interest|individual or group","Who is affected by, or can affect, its activities|affected|affect|influence",{model:"A stakeholder is any individual or group with an interest in a business, who is affected by, or can affect, its activities.",tip:"Don't confuse stakeholders with shareholders."}],
["1.4","Outline",2,"Outline how a business could reduce conflict between its stakeholders.","Communicate with and consult the groups involved|communicate|consult|negotiate|negotiation|meetings","Reach a compromise, for example sharing profits or agreeing priorities|compromise|profit sharing|share|priorities",{model:"Managers can consult the groups involved and negotiate a compromise, for example a profit-sharing scheme that satisfies both shareholders and employees.",tip:"A concrete example of a compromise strengthens the answer."}],
["1.5","Define",2,"Define diseconomies of scale.","Rising average (unit) costs|average cost|unit cost|cost per unit|rise","As a business becomes too large, for example because of communication or coordination problems|too large|too big|grows|communication|coordination",{model:"Diseconomies of scale are rising average costs as a business becomes too large, for example because communication and coordination become harder.",tip:"Say what happens to average cost and why."}],
["1.5","Outline",2,"Outline one reason why a firm might grow through a merger rather than organically.","It is much faster than organic growth|faster|quicker|rapid","It gains new markets, skills or market share, and may reduce competition|new markets|market share|reduce competition|skills|economies of scale",{model:"A merger is much faster than organic growth and immediately brings new markets, skills or market share, and it can reduce competition.",tip:"Speed is the classic reason; add what is gained."}],
["1.6","Outline",2,"Outline why a business might become a multinational company.","To reach new markets and customers|new markets|customers|sales","To cut costs, for example using cheaper labour or avoiding tariffs|cheaper labour|cheap labour|costs|tariffs|trade barriers",{model:"A business may become multinational to sell to new markets abroad and to cut costs, for example by producing where labour is cheaper or inside trade barriers to avoid tariffs.",tip:"Market access and cost reduction are the two main motives."}],
["1.6","Explain",2,"Explain how a host government might limit the power of multinational companies.","Through regulations such as environmental and labour laws|laws|regulation|regulations","Through taxes, local-content rules or limits on sending profits home|tax|taxes|local content|repatriation|ownership",{model:"A host government can pass environmental and labour laws that MNCs must obey, and use taxes or local-content rules so that more of the benefit stays in the country.",tip:"Name specific tools the government can use."}],
["2.1","Outline",2,"Outline two methods of training employees.","On-the-job training: learning while working, e.g. shadowing|on the job|shadowing|while working","Off-the-job training: away from the workplace, e.g. a course|off the job|course|courses|away from","Induction training for new staff|induction",{model:"On-the-job training happens while working, for example shadowing an experienced colleague. Off-the-job training happens away from work, for example on a college course.",tip:"Two methods, each with an example."}],
["2.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between dismissal and redundancy.","Dismissal is when an employee is fired for misconduct or poor performance|fired|misconduct|performance|fault","Redundancy is when the job itself is no longer needed|no longer needed|job no longer|position|closure|cut",{model:"Dismissal is when an employee is fired because of their own misconduct or poor performance. Redundancy is when the job itself is no longer needed, through no fault of the employee.",tip:"The key difference is whose 'fault' it is."}],
["2.2","Define",2,"Define delegation.","Passing authority to a subordinate|authority|subordinate|pass|passing","To carry out a task, while the manager stays responsible for it|task|tasks|responsible|accountable",{model:"Delegation is passing authority to a subordinate to carry out a task, while the manager remains ultimately responsible.",tip:"Authority is passed on; responsibility is not."}],
["2.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between centralized and decentralized organizational structures.","In a centralized structure, decisions are made by a few senior managers at the top|top|senior|head office|few","In a decentralized structure, decision-making is spread to lower levels or branches|lower levels|spread|branches|local|delegated",{model:"In a centralized structure a few senior managers at head office make the decisions. In a decentralized structure decisions are delegated to lower levels or local branches.",tip:"Say who makes the decisions in each."}],
["2.3","Outline",2,"Outline the democratic leadership style.","The leader involves employees in decision-making|involves|consult|consults|participate","But usually makes the final decision, which can improve motivation and ideas|final decision|motivation|ideas",{model:"A democratic leader consults employees and involves them in decision-making, but usually makes the final decision. This can raise motivation and bring in more ideas.",tip:"Mention an effect on employees."}],
["2.3","Outline",2,"Outline situational leadership.","The leader adapts their style to fit the circumstances|adapts|adapt|changes style|flexible","Depending on the task and the employees' skills and experience|task|skills|experience|maturity|followers",{model:"Situational leaders adapt their style to the circumstances: for example, more direction for new, inexperienced staff and more delegation for skilled, experienced ones.",tip:"Give an example of the style changing."}],
["2.4","Outline",2,"Outline Herzberg's two-factor theory.","Hygiene factors, such as pay and conditions, prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate|hygiene|dissatisfaction|pay|conditions","Motivators, such as recognition, responsibility and achievement, increase motivation|motivators|recognition|responsibility|achievement",{model:"Herzberg said hygiene factors such as pay and working conditions prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate, while motivators such as recognition, responsibility and achievement actually increase motivation.",tip:"Explain both factors and their different effects."}],
["2.4","Explain",2,"Explain one reason why job enrichment can improve motivation.","It gives workers more challenging tasks with more responsibility|challenging|responsibility|responsible|varied","This meets higher-level needs such as esteem and self-actualization|self actualisation|self actualization|esteem|achievement|recognition",{model:"Job enrichment gives workers more challenging tasks with more responsibility, which meets higher-level needs such as esteem and self-actualization, so they are more motivated.",tip:"Link it to a motivation theory such as Maslow or Herzberg."}],
["2.5","Outline",2,"Outline Handy's power culture.","Power is concentrated in a few people at the centre|concentrated|centre|center|few people|central","Decisions are made quickly, and staff are judged on results|quickly|fast|results",{model:"In Handy's power culture, power is concentrated in a few people at the centre. Decisions are made quickly, and staff are judged on results rather than methods.",tip:"Describe where power lies and what that means in practice."}],
["2.5","Explain",2,"Explain how organizational culture can affect employee performance.","A strong, positive culture creates shared values and commitment|shared values|commitment|loyal|belong","Which can raise motivation and productivity, while a toxic culture lowers them|motivation|productivity|morale|turnover",{model:"A strong, positive culture gives employees shared values and a sense of belonging, which raises motivation and productivity. A toxic culture lowers morale and increases staff turnover.",tip:"Show both a positive and a negative effect."}],
["2.6","Outline",2,"Outline one advantage of using email for business communication.","It is fast and cheap, reaching many people at once|fast|quick|cheap|many people","It also provides a written record|record|written",{model:"Email is fast and cheap and can reach many people at once, and it leaves a written record that can be referred back to.",tip:"Outline = brief, but more than one word."}],
["2.6","Outline",2,"Outline why communication can be difficult in a large organization.","Long chains of command mean messages pass through many layers and get distorted|chain of command|layers|distort|hierarchy","More people, departments and locations make communication slower|slower|delays|locations|departments",{model:"In a large organization messages pass down long chains of command, so they can be distorted, and with many departments and locations, communication is slower.",tip:"Give two causes linked to size."}],
["2.7","Outline",2,"Outline the role of trade unions.","They represent workers in negotiations with employers|represent|negotiate|negotiations|collective bargaining","To improve pay, working conditions and job security|pay|conditions|security|rights",{model:"Trade unions represent workers in collective bargaining with employers, aiming to improve pay, working conditions and job security.",tip:"Who they represent and what they aim for."}],
["2.7","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a strike and a work-to-rule.","In a strike, employees stop working completely|stop working|refuse to work|walk out","In a work-to-rule, employees do only exactly what their contract requires|contract|only|minimum|exactly",{model:"In a strike, employees stop working completely. In a work-to-rule they keep working but do only exactly what their contract requires, slowing output.",tip:"One sentence for each form of action."}],
["3.1","Outline",2,"Outline why a start-up needs finance.","To buy capital items such as equipment and premises|equipment|premises|machinery|capital","To cover running costs, such as wages, before enough revenue comes in|running costs|working capital|wages|before",{model:"A start-up needs finance to buy capital items such as premises and equipment, and to cover running costs such as wages before it earns enough revenue.",tip:"Capital expenditure and running costs are the two needs."}],
["3.1","Define",2,"Define working capital.","Current assets minus current liabilities|current assets|current liabilities|minus","The money available for day-to-day running of the business|day to day|daily|short term|running",{model:"Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities: the money available to pay for the day-to-day running of the business.",tip:"Give the formula and what it means."}],
["3.2","Outline",2,"Outline one advantage and one disadvantage of using retained profit as a source of finance.","Advantage: there is no interest to pay and no loss of control|no interest|no repayment|no control|cheap|free","Disadvantage: there may not be enough, and shareholders receive less in dividends|not enough|insufficient|dividends|limited",{model:"Retained profit has no interest to pay and doesn't dilute ownership, but there may not be enough of it, and using it means paying shareholders lower dividends.",tip:"Label each side clearly."}],
["3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between share capital and loan capital.","Share capital comes from selling shares; it gives ownership and is not repaid|ownership|shares|not repaid|dividends","Loan capital is borrowed money that must be repaid with interest|repaid|interest|borrowed",{model:"Share capital is raised by selling shares: buyers become part-owners and it is not repaid. Loan capital is borrowed and must be repaid with interest.",tip:"Ownership vs repayment is the difference."}],
["3.3","Calculate",1,"Fixed costs are $2000 and the variable cost is $3 per unit. Calculate the total cost of making 400 units.","$3200|3200|3 200",{model:"Total cost = fixed costs + (variable cost × quantity) = 2000 + (3 × 400) = $3200.",tip:"Show the formula, then substitute."}],
["3.3","Define",2,"Define average cost.","Total cost divided by the number of units produced|total cost|divided|number of units|output","The cost per unit|per unit|each unit",{model:"Average cost is total cost divided by the number of units produced: the cost per unit.",tip:"Give the formula."}],
["3.4","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between current assets and non-current assets.","Current assets are short-term, held for less than a year, e.g. cash and inventory|short term|less than a year|under a year|cash|inventory|stock","Non-current assets are long-term, kept for more than a year, e.g. buildings and machinery|long term|more than a year|over a year|buildings|machinery|vehicles",{model:"Current assets are expected to be used or turned into cash within a year, such as inventory and cash. Non-current assets are kept for more than a year, such as buildings and machinery.",tip:"Time period plus an example of each."}],
["3.4","Calculate",1,"A machine costs $14 000 and has a residual value of $2000 after 4 years. Calculate the annual straight-line depreciation.","$3000 a year|3000|3 000",{model:"Annual depreciation = (cost − residual value) ÷ useful life = (14 000 − 2000) ÷ 4 = $3000 a year.",tip:"Subtract the residual value before dividing."}],
["3.5","Calculate",1,"Net profit is $30 000 and capital employed is $150 000. Calculate the return on capital employed (ROCE).","20%|20|0.2",{model:"ROCE = net profit ÷ capital employed × 100 = 30 000 ÷ 150 000 × 100 = 20%.",tip:"Ratios as percentages need the % sign."}],
["3.5","Outline",2,"Outline what the acid-test ratio measures and why it leaves out inventory.","It measures a firm's ability to pay its short-term debts from its most liquid assets|short term|liquid|liquidity|pay debts|current liabilities","Inventory is left out because it may be hard to sell quickly for cash|hard to sell|not liquid|cannot be sold quickly|slow",{model:"The acid-test ratio measures whether a firm can pay its short-term debts from its most liquid assets. Inventory is excluded because it may not be possible to sell it quickly for cash.",tip:"Two parts: what it measures and why inventory is excluded."}],
["3.6","Define",2,"Define creditor days.","The average number of days a business takes to pay its suppliers|pay suppliers|suppliers|pay its","Trade payables ÷ cost of sales × 365|payables|creditors|cost of sales|365",{model:"Creditor days is the average time a business takes to pay its suppliers: (trade payables ÷ cost of sales) × 365.",tip:"Give the formula as well as the meaning."}],
["3.6","Explain",2,"Explain why a high gearing ratio can be risky.","A large share of capital comes from loans, so interest payments are high|interest|loans|debt|borrowing","If interest rates rise or profits fall, the firm may struggle to repay|rates rise|struggle|repay|insolvency|profits fall",{model:"A highly geared firm relies heavily on loans, so it has high interest payments. If interest rates rise or profits fall, it may struggle to meet repayments.",tip:"Link the debt to what could go wrong."}],
["3.7","Outline",2,"Outline the purpose of a cash flow forecast.","It predicts future cash inflows and outflows|inflows|outflows|predicts|future","So cash shortages can be spotted in advance and finance such as an overdraft arranged|shortage|shortages|overdraft|plan|in advance",{model:"A cash flow forecast predicts future cash inflows and outflows, so managers can spot cash shortages in advance and arrange finance such as an overdraft.",tip:"Say what it shows and how it is used."}],
["3.7","Explain",2,"Explain how a business can be profitable but still run out of cash.","Sales are made on credit, so cash arrives later|credit|later|delay|debtors|receivables","Meanwhile it must pay suppliers and wages, or buy inventory, now|pay suppliers|wages|inventory|stock|outflows",{model:"A business can make profitable sales on credit, so the cash only arrives weeks later, while it must pay suppliers and wages now. Its cash can run out even though it is making a profit.",tip:"Profit is not cash: timing is the explanation."}],
["3.8","Calculate",1,"A project costs $40 000 and earns $10 000 a year. Calculate its payback period.","4 years|4",{model:"Payback period = 40 000 ÷ 10 000 = 4 years.",tip:"Include the unit: years."}],
["3.8","Outline",2,"Outline one advantage of the average rate of return (ARR) method.","It considers the profit over the project's whole life|whole life|all profit|lifetime|entire","And gives a percentage that can be compared with interest rates or other projects|percentage|compare|compared|interest rate",{model:"ARR considers the profit over the project's whole life, and it gives a percentage that can be compared with other projects or with interest rates.",tip:"Contrast it with payback, which ignores later profits."}],
["3.9","Outline",2,"Outline two reasons for setting budgets.","To control and monitor spending|control|monitor|spending","To set targets that motivate managers|targets|motivate|motivation","To plan and coordinate departments|plan|planning|coordinate|allocate",{model:"Budgets let managers control and monitor spending, and they set targets that can motivate managers to perform.",tip:"Two separate reasons, each in its own sentence."}],
["3.9","Outline",2,"Outline one limitation of budgets.","They can become inaccurate as conditions change|inaccurate|unrealistic|change|out of date","Which can demotivate staff or reduce flexibility|demotivate|inflexible|flexibility|rigid",{model:"Budgets are based on forecasts, so they can become unrealistic when conditions change, which can demotivate staff or make the business inflexible.",tip:"Say why the limitation happens and its effect."}],
["4.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a niche market and a mass market.","A niche market is a small, specific segment with particular needs|small|specific|specialised|specialized|segment","A mass market is large, with general products sold to many customers|large|general|many customers|broad|everyone",{model:"A niche market is a small, specific segment with particular needs, such as vegan running shoes. A mass market is large, with general products sold to many customers, such as bottled water.",tip:"Size and specificity, with examples."}],
["4.1","Outline",2,"Outline why market share matters to a business.","A higher share can bring economies of scale and lower costs|economies of scale|lower costs|costs","It signals market leadership and brand strength, giving more power over prices|leader|leadership|brand|power|bargaining|status",{model:"A high market share can bring economies of scale and lower costs, and it signals market leadership, giving the firm more power over prices and suppliers.",tip:"Give two separate benefits."}],
["4.2","Outline",2,"Outline the purpose of a product position map.","It shows how customers see products on two features, such as price and quality|two features|price|quality|axes|perception","Helping the firm find gaps in the market or see its competitors|gap|gaps|competitors",{model:"A position map plots how customers see products on two features, such as price and quality, helping the firm spot gaps in the market and see where its competitors are.",tip:"Mention the two axes and the gaps."}],
["4.2","Define",2,"Define a unique selling point (USP).","A feature that makes a product different from its competitors|different from competitors|different from rivals|differentiates|differentiate|stands out|sets it apart|distinguishes|unique feature","Giving customers a reason to buy it|reason to buy|reason to choose|choose it over|choose it instead|buy it instead|attracts customers|persuades customers|reason for customers",{model:"A USP is a feature that makes a product different from its competitors and gives customers a reason to choose it.",tip:"Differentiation plus why it matters."}],
["4.3","Outline",2,"Outline why sales forecasts may be inaccurate.","They are based on past data, which may not predict the future|past data|past|historical","Unexpected changes, such as a recession, a new competitor or a change in tastes, can occur|unexpected|recession|competitor|tastes|trends|external",{model:"Forecasts are based on past data, which may not reflect the future, and unexpected changes such as a recession or a new competitor can make them wrong.",tip:"Two reasons, ideally one internal and one external."}],
["4.3","Define",2,"Define a moving average in sales forecasting.","The mean of a fixed number of consecutive data points|consecutive|fixed number|periods|data points","Recalculated each period to smooth out fluctuations and show the trend|smooth|trend|fluctuations|seasonal",{model:"A moving average is the mean of a fixed number of consecutive data points, recalculated each period, which smooths out fluctuations to show the underlying trend.",tip:"Say what it's used for: revealing the trend."}],
["4.4","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between random sampling and quota sampling.","In random sampling, everyone in the population has an equal chance of being selected|equal chance|chance|lottery","In quota sampling, a set number of people from each segment is chosen by the researcher|set number|each segment|groups|proportion",{model:"In random sampling, everyone in the population has an equal chance of being chosen. In quota sampling, the researcher chooses a set number of people from each segment, such as age group.",tip:"How people are chosen is the difference."}],
["4.4","Outline",2,"Outline one limitation of secondary market research.","It may be out of date|out of date|outdated|old","It may not fit the firm's exact needs, and competitors can use it too|not specific|not suited|irrelevant|competitors|unreliable|bias",{model:"Secondary data may be out of date, and because it was collected for another purpose it may not fit the firm's exact needs, and competitors can use it too.",tip:"Explain why the limitation matters."}],
["4.5","Outline",2,"Outline the difference between price skimming and penetration pricing.","Skimming sets a high price at launch to target early adopters and recover costs|high price|high prices|high initial price|high launch price|premium price|charges high","Penetration pricing sets a low price to gain market share quickly|low price|low prices|low initial price|lower price|charges low|market share",{model:"Price skimming sets a high launch price to target early adopters and recover development costs. Penetration pricing sets a low price to win market share quickly.",tip:"High vs low price, with the aim of each."}],
["4.5","Outline",2,"Outline the stages of the product life cycle.","Introduction (launch) and growth|introduction|launch|growth","Maturity (saturation) and decline|maturity|saturation|decline",{model:"A product goes through introduction (launch), growth, maturity (saturation) and decline.",tip:"List all the stages in order."}],
["4.6","Outline",2,"Outline one cultural difference that can affect international marketing.","Differences in language, religion, customs or tastes|language|religion|customs|tastes|values|traditions","So products, names or adverts may need adapting|adapt|adapting|change|translate|offensive",{model:"Countries differ in language and customs, so a brand name or advert may need translating or adapting to avoid causing offence.",tip:"Name the difference, then its effect on marketing."}],
["4.6","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a global (standardized) and a localized marketing strategy.","A global strategy uses the same product and marketing everywhere, giving economies of scale|same|everywhere|economies of scale","A localized strategy adapts the marketing mix to each country's tastes and culture|adapts|adapting|tastes|culture|each country",{model:"A global strategy uses the same product and marketing everywhere, which gives economies of scale. A localized strategy adapts the marketing mix to each country's tastes and culture.",tip:"Standardise vs adapt, with a benefit of each."}],
["5.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between labour productivity and capital productivity.","Labour productivity is output per worker (or per hour worked)|per worker|output per worker|per employee|per hour","Capital productivity is output per unit of capital, such as per machine|per machine|per unit of capital",{model:"Labour productivity is the output per worker, or per hour worked. Capital productivity is the output per unit of capital, such as per machine.",tip:"Both are output per input: name the input."}],
["5.1","Outline",2,"Outline one way a business can increase productivity.","For example training workers, using new technology or automation, or better incentives|training|technology|automation|machinery|motivation|incentives","So more output is produced from the same inputs|more output|same inputs|efficiency|output per",{model:"Training workers or investing in automation lets the business produce more output from the same inputs, so productivity rises.",tip:"Name a method and link it to more output."}],
["5.2","Define",2,"Define cell production.","Production is organized into small teams (cells), each responsible for a complete unit or stage|teams|team|group|cells","Which can improve motivation and quality|motivation|quality|ownership|responsibility",{model:"In cell production, the work is organized into small teams, each responsible for a complete unit or stage, which can improve motivation and quality.",tip:"Add why businesses use it."}],
["5.2","Outline",2,"Outline one advantage of mass production.","Low unit costs from economies of scale|low cost|lower cost|economies of scale|cheap|unit cost","With consistent quality and a high output|consistent|standard|high output|large quantities|fast",{model:"Mass production gives low unit costs through economies of scale, with high output of consistent, standardized products.",tip:"Cost advantage is the main point."}],
["5.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between quality control and quality assurance.","Quality control inspects finished products to find defects|inspect|inspection|finished|end|defects","Quality assurance builds quality into every stage of production to prevent defects|every stage|throughout|prevent|process",{model:"Quality control inspects finished products to catch defects. Quality assurance builds quality into every stage of production so that defects are prevented in the first place.",tip:"Detect vs prevent."}],
["5.3","Outline",2,"Outline total quality management (TQM).","Every employee is responsible for quality|every employee|everyone|all employees|all staff","With a continuous focus on meeting customer needs and improving|continuous|customer|improvement|improve",{model:"TQM makes every employee responsible for quality, with a continuous focus on meeting customers' needs and improving processes.",tip:"Everyone, continuously, for the customer."}],
["5.4","Outline",2,"Outline one reason for reshoring.","Rising costs abroad, or quality and supply-chain problems|rising costs|wages rise|quality problems|supply chain|delays","Bringing production closer to customers improves control and reputation|closer|control|image|made in|reputation",{model:"Firms reshore when wages abroad rise or supply chains cause delays and quality problems. Producing closer to home improves control and can boost reputation.",tip:"Cause and benefit."}],
["5.4","Outline",2,"Outline one disadvantage of outsourcing.","Loss of control over quality or delivery|loss of control|control|quality","Risks to reputation, confidentiality or jobs|reputation|confidentiality|redundancies|job losses|morale",{model:"Outsourcing means less control over quality and delivery, and poor work by the supplier can damage the firm's reputation.",tip:"Give the disadvantage and its consequence."}],
["5.5","Calculate",2,"The price is $20, the variable cost is $12 per unit and fixed costs are $4000. Calculate the contribution per unit and the break-even quantity.","Contribution per unit = $8|8","Break-even quantity = 500 units|500",{model:"Contribution per unit = 20 − 12 = $8. Break-even quantity = fixed costs ÷ contribution = 4000 ÷ 8 = 500 units.",tip:"Contribution first, then divide fixed costs by it."}],
["5.5","Define",2,"Define the margin of safety.","The amount by which actual output (sales) exceeds the break-even quantity|exceeds|above|actual|minus|difference","Showing how far sales could fall before a loss is made|fall|before a loss|loss|cushion",{model:"The margin of safety is the amount by which actual sales exceed the break-even quantity: how far sales could fall before the business makes a loss.",tip:"Give the formula and what it shows."}],
["5.6","Define",2,"Define lead time.","The time between ordering stock and it being delivered|ordering|order is placed|delivered|delivery|arrives","It is used to set the reorder level|reorder|re order",{model:"Lead time is the time between placing an order for stock and it being delivered; it is used to set the reorder level.",tip:"Link it to the reorder level."}],
["5.6","Calculate",1,"A factory can make 5000 units a month but makes 4000. Calculate its capacity utilization.","80%|80|0.8",{model:"Capacity utilization = actual output ÷ maximum output × 100 = 4000 ÷ 5000 × 100 = 80%.",tip:"Actual over maximum, times 100."}],
["5.7","Outline",2,"Outline why contingency planning can be costly.","Time and money are spent planning for events that may never happen|may never happen|never happen|might not happen","Plans need regular updating, and staff need training|updating|update|training|drills|out of date",{model:"Contingency planning uses time and money on events that may never happen, and the plans need regular updating and staff training.",tip:"Explain the cost, not just say it is expensive."}],
["5.7","Outline",2,"Outline two features of effective crisis management.","A fast response|fast|quick|speed|rapid","Clear, honest communication with stakeholders and the media|communication|transparency|transparent|honest|media","Strong leadership and control|leadership|control|leader",{model:"Effective crisis management needs a fast response and clear, honest communication with stakeholders and the media.",tip:"Two features, each a separate sentence."}],
["5.8","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between product innovation and process innovation.","Product innovation creates new or improved goods or services|new product|new goods|improved products|goods","Process innovation improves the way goods are produced or delivered|way|method|methods|production|delivered",{model:"Product innovation creates new or improved goods and services. Process innovation improves the way goods are produced or delivered.",tip:"What is made vs how it is made."}],
["5.8","Outline",2,"Outline one reason why research and development can be risky.","It is expensive and the outcome is uncertain|expensive|costly|uncertain|may fail|fail","Competitors may copy the idea, or customers may not want the result|copy|competitors|patent|demand|customers",{model:"R&D is expensive and the results are uncertain: the product may fail, or competitors may copy the idea.",tip:"Cost plus uncertainty is the core risk."}],
["5.9","Outline",2,"Outline one benefit of using artificial intelligence in management information systems.","It can analyse large amounts of data quickly|large amounts|big data|quickly|fast|analyse","To find patterns, predict demand or automate decisions|patterns|predict|forecast|automate|decisions",{model:"AI can analyse very large amounts of data quickly to find patterns, for example predicting demand, so managers make better decisions.",tip:"Say what AI does and how managers benefit."}],
["5.9","Outline",2,"Outline one risk of cybercrime for a business.","Data breaches can expose customer or company data|breach|stolen|theft|hack|hacked|ransomware","Leading to financial loss, fines or damage to reputation|financial loss|fines|reputation|trust|lawsuits",{model:"A hacker could steal customer data in a breach, leading to fines, lawsuits and a loss of customer trust.",tip:"The risk plus its consequence."}],
]);
